  "problem": "For the sets $X={k,m,n}$ and $Z=X\\times X,$ determine which of the following statements are true.\n\n- ${k}^{2}\\in Z$\n- $(k,n,m)\\in Z$\n- $(m,n)\\in Z$",
  "worked_solution": "The Cartesian product $X\\times X$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $X$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $X.$\n\nIn this case, the schematic for ${X}^{2}=X\\times X$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040355/assets/e7c24108e0d9f5e2e7edbfbe90cdf7318bc247aa73a4c9c72dc61449c7b4da94.png)\n\nHence, the set $Z=X\\times X$ is given by\n\n$$\nZ={(k,k),(k,m),(k,n),(m,k),(m,m),(m,n),(n,k),(n,m),(n,n)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"",
